diff --git a/conformance/xaravel-consequences/contracts.jsonl b/conformance/xaravel-consequences/contracts.jsonl
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4246052
--- /dev/null
+++ b/conformance/xaravel-consequences/contracts.jsonl
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+{"id":"AD-1.1","text":"**Contents.** Every `Illuminate\\Contracts\\*` interface is ported into `@xaravel/contracts` at the subpath of its Upstream namespace (`Illuminate\\Contracts\\Mail\\Mailer` → `@xaravel/contracts/mail`). Each contract exports an `interface` and a runtime **token** under the same name. The token's brand key is `Symbol.for('<Upstream FQCN>')`, so `@xaravel/runtime` and the grader can detect it without importing contracts. The interface requires `readonly [Token]: true`, and the token's `Symbol.hasInstance` reads that brand, so `x instanceof Arrayable` works as in PHP and `implements` forces the brand at compile time (brand placement: AD-9)."}
+{"id":"AD-1.2","text":"**Concrete types inside contracts.** Where an Upstream contract names a concrete class (`Request`, `Response`, `Collection`), the TS contract uses a structural interface declared in `@xaravel/contracts`. It never imports the implementation."}
+{"id":"AD-1.3","text":"**Container keys.** Framework code injects contracts by token. The container's public API accepts `string | token | class` abstracts exactly as Upstream does."}
+{"id":"AD-1.4","text":"**Alias table.** `@xaravel/contracts/aliases` ports Upstream's `registerCoreContainerAliases` and each provider's string bindings verbatim, as data: alias string → FQCN string. The container resolves a class argument through `fqcn(class)` and then this table. Every service has one **canonical token**: the most specific contract it is aliased to (`CacheManager` → `Cache\\Factory`). Providers bind only the canonical token. Concrete-class tokens, other contracts, the Upstream FQCN string and short aliases (`'cache'`) are aliases of it, so autowiring a listed concrete class never builds a second instance."}
+{"id":"AD-1.5","text":"**App and package interfaces.** Their tokens are made with `contract<T>('<FQCN>')`. Abstract classes are tokens as-is. Importing a contract with `import type` for injection is a lint error."}
+{"id":"AD-1.6","text":"**Phase and protection.** The package is completed in Phase 1, together with every Xaravel-only contract listed in AD-27. It is verified by Xaravel Tests plus the API-surface check (AD-15), and is Protected after Phase 1."}
+{"id":"AD-2.1","text":"**Upstream edges.** For each Upstream Component, `illuminate/*` packages in `require` become `dependencies` (`workspace:*`). `illuminate/*` packages in `suggest` become optional `peerDependencies`, reachable only through a container token or alias or a guarded `import()`."}
+{"id":"AD-2.2","text":"**Always allowed.** Any package may depend on `@xaravel/runtime` and `@xaravel/contracts`."}
+{"id":"AD-2.3","text":"**Third-party code.** Third-party `require` entries follow AD-22."}
+{"id":"AD-2.4","text":"**CI checks.** CI fails on a dependency cycle, and on a static import of a package not listed in `dependencies`. Each package is type-checked as its own `tsc` project reference with `types` limited to its dependencies."}
+{"id":"AD-2.5","text":"**Foundation.** It statically imports only Components of its own or earlier Phases. Default providers, middleware priority and later Components are FQCN strings, resolved through the Class Registry, and a provider whose package isn't installed is skipped. A later package adds itself through package discovery, and never edits Foundation. Discovery works as in Laravel: - It reads `package.json` → `\"xaravel\": { \"providers\": [...], \"aliases\": {...}, \"dontDiscover\": [...] }`. `aliases` means Laravel's facade aliases, not container aliases. - `package:discover` caches the result to `bootstrap/cache/packages.json`. - Providers load in this order: framework defaults, then discovered providers, then `bootstrap/providers.ts`."}
+{"id":"AD-2.6","text":"**Meta-package.** `@xaravel/framework` lists every optional peer as a real dependency."}
+{"id":"AD-2.7","text":"**Grader.** Graded packages take the grader only as a `devDependency`. There are two exceptions: `@xaravel/testing` may depend on `@xaravel/mockery` at runtime (FR-100), and `@xaravel/support` takes it as an optional peer for `Facade.shouldReceive` / `spy` / `partialMock`."}
+{"id":"AD-2.8","text":"**Tests.** Test files form their own `tsc` project. They may import grader packages and any package of the same or an earlier Phase, through `devDependencies`. Dev edges are excluded from the cycle check. A Test ID that needs a later-Phase package is `blocked-by:<that package>`."}
+{"id":"AD-2.9","text":"**Scaffolding.** A Phase 0 Infrastructure Story scaffolds every package: `package.json`, a generated `tsconfig.json` that extends the Protected `tsconfig.base.json`, `src/`, `tests/`, and the root project references. Afterwards, Stories may edit their package's own `package.json`. CI checks that each package `tsconfig.json` equals its generated form."}
+{"id":"AD-2.10","text":"**Boost framework mode.** It is the private workspace `tools/boost-framework`. Its only dependency is `@modelcontextprotocol/server`, and it reads `conformance/`, `stories/` and `decisions/` read-only."}
+{"id":"AD-6.1","text":"**Async roots.** These are the only async Driver-contract methods: `Cache\\Store`, `Session` handlers, `Queue\\Queue`, `Mail` transports, `Filesystem` IO methods, database statement methods, Redis commands, HTTP-client sending, `Broadcaster` broadcast and auth, `Process` runs. Within those contracts, a method is async if and only if at least one Upstream driver performs IO in it. So `Storage.url`, `temporaryUrl` presigning and `Cache.getPrefix` stay sync. `Hasher`, `Encrypter`, `Translator`, view loading and Config are sync. Adding a root needs an Escalation."}
+{"id":"AD-6.2","text":"**Derivation.** A public method is async if and only if it reaches a root, or it accepts a callback that DIV-1 allows to be async. The second clause covers gates, policies, `Gate.before/after`, listeners, observers, model events, validation closures and rule objects, `Cache.remember*`, transaction callbacks, route-binding resolvers, middleware, limiters and `afterResolvingAsync` hooks. That makes `Gate.allows/denies/check/any/authorize/inspect`, `can()`, `Validator.passes/fails/validate/validated`, `Event.dispatch/until`, and model `save/delete/update/push` async. A method that is async on any path is async on all paths, and a `T | Promise<T>` return type is a lint error."}
+{"id":"AD-6.3","text":"**Loaded-once state is sync.** - **Session:** after `StartSession`, which also covers `old()` and `$errors`. - **Request input:** after the body is loaded (AD-12's pre-body stage). - **The authenticated user:** a step after `StartSession`, and each `auth:<guard>` middleware, resolves its guard's user into the frame. After that, `request.user()`, `Auth.user/check/guest/id` and Blade `@auth` / `@guest` are sync. Reading an unresolved guard throws `AuthGuardNotResolvedException`, which points to `await Auth.guard('<name>').authenticate()`."}
+{"id":"AD-6.4","text":"**Container hooks.** `resolving` / `afterResolving` callbacks are sync-only. `afterResolvingAsync(brand, fn)` hooks run only through `Container.callAsync` and the route dispatcher, after parameter resolution and before the action. FormRequest validation registers there."}
+{"id":"AD-6.5","text":"**Dual helpers.** `retry`, `rescue`, `tap`, `value`, `transform`, `once`, `when` / `unless`, `Benchmark.measure` and `Sleep` return `T` for a sync callback and `Promise<T>` for an async one."}
+{"id":"AD-6.6","text":"**Fake and `TestResponse` assertions.** They accept boolean-or-Promise predicates, and return `Promise<void>` when a predicate is given (DIV-8)."}
+{"id":"AD-6.7","text":"**Logger.** It is sync. Channels buffer, and flush through their Driver asynchronously. `MessageLogged` listeners must be sync."}
+{"id":"AD-6.8","text":"**Sync callbacks.** Sync APIs type their callbacks as `NotPromise` overloads (`map`, `each`, `filter`, `reduce`, `sortBy`, …), and lint enforces the rest. The async companions are `eachAsync`, `mapAsync`, `filterAsync`, `rejectAsync`, `firstAsync`, `everyAsync`, `containsAsync` and `partitionAsync`."}
+{"id":"AD-6.9","text":"**Thenables.** Builders, Relations and `PendingRequest` are thenable: awaiting one runs its terminal. A thenable returned from an `async` function is a lint error, in the framework and in the Skeleton's lint config. Framework packages and Ported Tests enforce it with the type-aware `typescript/return-await` rule set to `always` (so `return this.query()` is caught), pinned in `lint:disables`; the Skeleton keeps the syntactic `xaravel/no-async-thenable-return` rule."}
+{"id":"AD-6.10","text":"**Destructor terminals.** `PendingDispatch`, `PendingChain` / `PendingBatch` dispatch, `PendingBroadcast` and `PendingCommand` start their terminal in a microtask after construction, so chained calls made synchronously still apply. The current frame tracks the terminal and awaits it before the unit ends. Awaiting the object returns the same Promise, so the terminal runs exactly once. Creating one outside a frame throws."}
+{"id":"AD-6.11","text":"**Lazy collections.** `cursor()` and `lazy()` return `AsyncLazyCollection` (`LazyCollection`'s method names, consumed with `for await`)."}
+{"id":"AD-6.12","text":"**IO order.** Framework IO is awaited sequentially in Upstream's order. `Promise.all` is allowed only where Upstream is itself concurrent (`Http.pool`, `Concurrency.run`, `Process.pool`, batch dispatch), and each such use is listed in `PORTING.md`."}
+{"id":"AD-6.13","text":"**Enforcement.** - Every async public method carries a docblock tag `@asyncRoot <Contract.method>` (or `@asyncRoot callback` for the callback clause). JSDoc survives `.d.ts` emission. - Each package commits `async-surface.json`, generated by a `tools/` script that walks the emitted `.d.ts` with `oxc-parser`, using the thenable type list in `@xaravel/contracts/async`. It has one line per async method, with the root taken from the tag, and one `thenable` line per sync method that returns a thenable type, which carries no tag because it is not async. Thenables are matched by qualified name, never by short name. - CI fails on a missing tag or on drift. The merge queue (AD-28) regenerates and checks the file. Fidelity Review checks that each tag is true. - oxlint type-aware rules run with `no-floating-promises` (`checkThenables: true`), `await-thenable` and `no-misused-promises` (`checksVoidReturn: true`, `checksConditionals: true`; ESC-6.1-1), over packages, Ported Tests, the Skeleton and compiled-view snapshots."}
+{"id":"AD-11.1","text":"**Compilation.** The View compiler emits TypeScript, which `Bun.Transpiler` turns into an ES module cached at `storage/framework/views/<hash>.js` (`view:cache` precompiles). Each module exports `render(ctx: RenderContext): Promise<string>` and `fragments: Record<string, (ctx) => Promise<string>>`. `RenderContext`, which lives in `@xaravel/contracts/view`, carries the data, the factory, the per-render state (sections, stacks, `@once`, the component stack, the loop stack) and a fragment capture set."}
+{"id":"AD-11.2","text":"**Variables [ADOPTED].** Templates use PHP's `$` names (`{{ $user.name }}`, `$loop`, `$errors`, `$slot`, `$attributes`, `$component`). The compiler destructures every free `$identifier` from the render data, and an undeclared name is `undefined`, so `@isset` and `@empty` stay safe. Other free identifiers resolve to helpers and facades. Data is passed without the `$`: `view('x', { user })`."}
+{"id":"AD-11.3","text":"**Expressions.** Expressions are TypeScript and may `await`. Output goes through Upstream's `e()`. Printing a Promise or thenable throws `ViewException`. Every directive condition and iterable is wrapped in a guard that throws on a thenable. `@can` compiles to an awaited check."}
+{"id":"AD-11.4","text":"**Fragments.** `view().fragment(...)` follows Upstream: the whole view renders with capture. A direct fragment call is allowed only when the compiler marks the fragment closed: it reads no template locals defined outside it and has no stack or `@once` effects."}
+{"id":"AD-11.5","text":"**Custom directives.** Handlers return TypeScript source that uses the emitted-code helpers named in `@xaravel/contracts/view`. That is the fixed ABI for `Blade.directive`, `Blade.if` and `Blade.stringable`."}
+{"id":"AD-11.6","text":"**Views as strings.** `View#toString()` throws a `ViewException` that names `await view(...).render()`. Inside a template, `{!! view(...) !!}` is awaited."}
+{"id":"AD-16.1","text":"**The list.** `conformance/protected-paths` (globs, itself Protected) is mirrored into `CODEOWNERS` with the Maintainer as owner. It covers: - in `conformance/`: everything except `expectations/`, `escalations/`, `oracle-requests/` and the signed `fixtures/`; - `decisions/`, `PORTING.md` and `stories/`; - `.github/` and `.devcontainer/` (the reference environment's pins, AD-17); - `tools/`; - `packages/assert`, `packages/mockery` and `packages/contracts` (contracts after Phase 1); - the root `package.json`, `bun.lock`, the root `bunfig.toml`, the root `tsconfig*.json` and `tsconfig.base.json`, `.oxlintrc.json`, `.gitattributes`, `.bun-version` and the root `LICENSE`. Package `tsconfig.json` files are generated and checked (AD-2). - the agent instructions and tooling: `AGENTS.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md` in any letter case (none may exist; one would stop Claude Code reading `AGENTS.md`), `.claude/` (the settings, hooks and skills agents run, such as `bmad-build-auto`), and `.mcp.json` (the MCP servers agent sessions load, such as Boost framework mode; ESC-7.4-1)."}
+{"id":"AD-16.2","text":"**Enforcement.** A GitHub ruleset on `main` requires the `protected-paths` and `ratchet` checks. They come from workflows pinned to the default branch (`pull_request_target`, which treats the PR diff as data only), so a PR cannot change its own checks. A change to a Protected Path needs Maintainer Code Owner review. That review is the Infrastructure Story approval and replaces any label."}
+{"id":"AD-16.3","text":"**Permissions.** Workflows default to `permissions: contents: read`. Write scopes are granted per job to the Oracle, dashboard and release jobs only. Agents push with an identity limited to `story/*` branches, with no ruleset bypass."}
+{"id":"AD-16.4","text":"**Disabling lint.** An `oxlint-disable` of any rule this spine names fails CI."}
+{"id":"AD-16.5","text":"**Story manifest.** Story files are `stories/<story-id>.md`, written by sprint planning. CI reads `test_ids:` and `component:` from the **base branch**. A Story branch is named `story/<story-id>`. A Test ID may be listed by at most one open Story. CI fails a PR that changes Expectations lines or `portedTest` blocks whose IDs are not in its `test_ids`."}
+{"id":"AD-16.6","text":"**Unexpected passes.** An unexpected pass of a Test ID owned by no open Story is a warning, and a bot opens a promotion PR. One owned by another open Story fails only that Story's next run."}
+{"id":"AD-16.7","text":"**Planning PRs.** A `planning/*` branch may change tooling, docs, decisions and planning records, but never `packages/**` or `conformance/expectations/**`: framework code and grader data change only through `story/*` branches. The ownership check enforces this (decisions/MAINT-2026-10-17-epic-1-items.md). Through Phase 0 only, a retrospective fix on `planning/retro-<N>-*` (N ≤ 8) may change `packages/**`, never Expectations (decisions/MAINT-2026-10-16-unattended-orchestrator.md, amendment 2026-10-18)."}
+{"id":"AD-16.8","text":"**Bot PRs.** Quarantine and promotion bots push `bot/*` branches as their own App identity. The ownership check exempts them when they change only Expectations `status` values, and they open `ESC-bot-<n>` Escalations."}
+{"id":"AD-16.9","text":"**Escalations.** An agent writes `conformance/escalations/ESC-<story-id>-<n>.md`, with frontmatter `test_ids`, `proposed` and `evidence`. The ruling is `decisions/ESC-<story-id>-<n>.md` with `decision: approved | rejected`. An Escalation counts as approved only when its decision file exists."}
+{"id":"AD-25.1","text":"**Hook order.** `@xaravel/contracts/testing` fixes the order of the hooks: `createApplication` → `afterApplicationCreated` → `setUp<Trait>` per trait → test → `tearDown<Trait>` → `beforeApplicationDestroyed` → test frame ends."}
+{"id":"AD-25.2","text":"**Frame order.** The harness opens the test frame first, through the runtime protocol. `createApplication` then builds and boots a fresh Application inside it, as in Laravel, writing to that Application's unsealed base. The Application is sealed before the test body runs. The `RefreshDatabase` lease (AD-20) and `setUp<Trait>` writes belong to the test frame."}
+{"id":"AD-25.3","text":"**Shared-app opt-in.** A suite that doesn't vary config before boot may instead boot one Application in `beforeAll`, outside any test frame. Each test frame then overlays it."}
+{"id":"AD-25.4","text":"**Resets.** All NFR-7 state is frame-scoped, so discarding the test frame resets it. Each item has a Xaravel Test proving its reset."}
+{"id":"AD-25.5","text":"**App tests [ADOPTED].** Pest-style: `uses(TestCase, RefreshDatabase)`, then `test(name, async function () { ... })` with `this` bound to the TestCase. Arrow-function test bodies in files that call `uses()` are a lint error. HTTP and console test calls return a thenable `PendingTestResponse` / `PendingCommand` whose `assert*` calls queue until the result arrives: `await this.post(url, data).assertRedirect(...).assertSessionHas('ok')`. `make:test` emits this form."}
+{"id":"AD-25.6","text":"**Ported Tests.** They use the same TestCase classes through `portedTest`'s `case`."}
+{"id":"AD-25.7","text":"**Testbench.** Its `TestCase` extends Foundation's and only adds hooks (`defineEnvironment`, `getPackageProviders`, `WithConfig`, `WithEnv`), which run before providers register."}
+{"id":"AD-27.1","text":"**Phase 0:** the Conformance System, `@xaravel/assert`, `@xaravel/mockery`, `tools/lint-rules` and `tools/boost-framework`. Also the Upstream scans at the Pin: DIV-12 pairs, facade `@method` lists, the `singleton()` list, the closure allowed-identifier list and API-surface extraction. Also scaffolding for every package (AD-2)."}
+{"id":"AD-27.2","text":"**Phase 1:** `@xaravel/runtime` first (scope, registry, values, codecs), then `@xaravel/contracts`, including every Xaravel-only contract: the alias table, the async thenable list, the view ABI and the test lifecycle. Seams are owned by their host packages (AD-12)."}
+{"id":"AD-27.3","text":"**Phase 3:** `@xaravel/http-foundation`, `@xaravel/mime`, `@xaravel/finder`, and `@xaravel/serializable-closure` before Bus."}
